# Getting started with Slap

```elixir
Mix.install([{:slap, "~> 0.7.0"}])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook has a short example for each Slap API: SlateDB, Streams,
snapshot logs, Yjs, KV and files. It requires Elixir 1.18 or later and
Erlang/OTP 26.2 or later. Run the cells in order. They keep data in memory,
so it is lost when the runtime stops.

To run it without Livebook, paste the cells into IEx started with
`iex -e 'Mix.install([{:slap, "~> 0.7.0"}])'`.

## SlateDB

Open a database, write a value and wait until it is durable, read it back,
and close the database:

```elixir
{:ok, db} = Slap.SlateDB.open("example", store: :memory)
{:ok, _seq} = Slap.SlateDB.put(db, "user:1", "Ada", await_durable: true)
{:ok, "Ada"} = Slap.SlateDB.get(db, "user:1")
:ok = Slap.SlateDB.close(db)
```

## Start the services

`Slap.start_link/1` starts a supervisor, which starts each service listed
in the options: here `streams`, `yjs`, `kv` and `files`. Yjs stores its
documents in Streams, and Files stores its records in KV. The Streams and KV
clusters get one shard each:

```elixir
{:ok, _} =
  Slap.start_link(
    store: :memory,
    streams: [shards: 1],
    yjs: [],
    kv: [shards: 1],
    files: []
  )
```

Run this cell only once: running it again fails with
`{:error, {:already_started, pid}}`. To start over, restart the runtime
from Livebook's Runtime panel and run the cells in order. In IEx, start a
new session.

The [architecture guide](https://hexdocs.pm/slap/architecture.html) shows
what `Slap` starts and how the packages depend on each other.

## Streams

Create, append to, and read a stream:

```elixir
{:ok, :created, _} = Slap.Streams.create("/events", content_type: "text/plain")
{:ok, %{result: :appended}} = Slap.Streams.append("/events", "created")
{:ok, %{messages: [{0, "created"}]}} = Slap.Streams.read("/events", 0)
```

## Snapshot logs

A snapshot log is an append-only log, stored in Streams, whose prefix can be
replaced by a snapshot. A snapshot at an offset must contain the effects of
every entry before that offset; the log cannot check this. Publishing the
snapshot trims those entries:

```elixir
base = "/v1/stream/examples/log"
{:ok, _} = Slap.SnapshotLog.append(base, "one")
{:ok, %{entries: ["one"], offset: offset}} = Slap.SnapshotLog.next(base, 0, wait: 0)
:ok = Slap.SnapshotLog.snapshot(base, offset, "state after one")
{:reset, %{snapshot: "state after one", offset: ^offset}} = Slap.SnapshotLog.next(base, nil)
```

A new consumer calls `next(base, nil)` to get the current snapshot and the
offset to read later entries from.

## Yjs

Define a document server module, join a document, and edit it with `y_ex`:

```elixir
defmodule Example.DocServer do
  use Slap.Yjs.DocServer
end

{:ok, server} = Slap.Yjs.Docs.join(Example.DocServer, {"examples", "doc-1"})
{:ok, doc} = Slap.Yjs.DocServer.doc(server, 5_000)
text = Yex.Doc.get_text(doc, "text")
:ok = Yex.Text.insert(text, 0, "hello")
```

Call `sync/2` to wait for the document's buffered changes to be stored, then
read the text:

```elixir
:ok = Slap.Yjs.DocServer.sync(server, 5_000)
"hello" = Yex.Text.to_string(text)
```

## KV

Write a row only if it does not exist yet, then read it:

```elixir
{:ok, version} = Slap.KV.put("users", "42", "Ada", if_version: :absent)
{:ok, %{value: "Ada", version: ^version}} = Slap.KV.get("users", "42")
```

A conditional write fails if the version it names is no longer current.
The error includes the current version:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Slap.KV.put("users", "42", "Grace", if_version: version)
{:error, {:conflict, _current}} = Slap.KV.put("users", "42", "Katherine", if_version: version)
```

## Files

Files use the KV cluster. Small bodies are stored in KV records:

```elixir
ref = {"examples", "hello.txt"}
{:ok, %{storage: :inline}} = Slap.Files.put(ref, "hello", if_version: :absent)
{:ok, "hello"} = Slap.Files.read(ref)
```

